# Meta-regression heterogeneity — Domain with majority-male authors in O and R

## Final hypothesis (data-grounded, candidate)
> **Economics** is the only domain in the meta-regression replication-projects
> data where the majority of authors are male **in both the original and the
> replication study**. Economists' original papers are 87.0% male (mean
> `authors_male.o` = 0.870, median 1.000, n=18) and the replications are 83.7%
> male (mean `authors_male.r` = 0.837, median 1.000); 55.6% of the O/R pairs in
> Economics have **strict male majority in both** stages (> 0.5) and 83.3%
> reach the ≥ 0.5 threshold in both. The two psychology disciplines
> (Cognitive n=234: mean_r = 0.514; Social n=649: mean_r = 0.504) hover right
> at parity on the replication side.
> *Caveat: the Economics evidence rests on n = 18 O/R pairs (single project,
> Experimental Economics).*

## Primary slice — `aggregated != True` (n = 901, drops 26 re-aggregated dup rows)

