Notes: wmean_X = Σ(n_authors.X · authors_male.X) / Σ n_authors.X (within discipline). `share_both > 0.5` = fraction of O/R pairs in the discipline with both `authors_male.o > 0.5` and `authors_male.r > 0.5`.

## Sensitivity — confirms ranking is invariant
- **SENS-A: aggregated ∈ {False, True}** (n=812, excludes Economics by construction). Cognitive mean_r = 0.5133, share_both_gt05 = 0.4322; Social mean_r = 0.5080, share_both_gt05 = 0.3132. Without Economics, no discipline has mean_r materially above 0.5.
- **SENS-B: ALL 927 rows** Economics unchanged (mean_o 0.8704, mean_r 0.8370, share_both_gt05 0.5556); Cognitive (0.7731 / 0.5142 / 0.4108) and Social (0.7343 / 0.5055 / 0.3009) shift only marginally.

## Validation
- 927 rows / 141 unique ids. The 26 `aggregated == True` rows exactly overlap 26 `aggregated == False` ids (True ∩ False = 26 of 26, True ∩ NaN = ∅), confirming True rows are re-aggregated duplicates of specific studies. They were dropped to avoid double counting.
- 115 `aggregated == NaN` rows have unique ids (no duplication); all 18 Economics rows are in this group (project = ee).
- `authors_male.o` and `authors_male.r` are ratios in [0, 1] with no missing values (n = 927 each).

## Final answer
**Economics** (project *ee*, Experimental Economics). Numerics above establish Economics (n=18) as the only discipline whose mean (and median) `authors_male.r` clearly exceeds the male-majority 0.5 threshold and whose majority-of-pairs share for both O and R is the largest of the three disciplines (55.6% strictly > 0.5; 83.3% ≥ 0.5). Caveat: small sample, single project.
